abstract: >-
  Two Lean 4 proofs of the class number one theorem for imaginary quadratic fields, each accepted by two independent kernels
  (Lean's and nanoda) through Comparator in local, unsandboxed runs: for squarefree m >= 1, the field Q(sqrt(-m)) has class
  number one if and only if m is in {1, 2, 3, 7, 11, 19, 43, 67, 163}. Neither uses Heegner, Baker, Stark or Gross-Zagier input,
  and both prove the same Lean statement. The first goes through OpenAI's zero-free region Re s > 7/8 for Dirichlet L-functions,
  whose Lean proof is inside the checked closure, and the Chowla-Selberg formula at s = 7/8, which gives the explicit bound
  d <= 652 proved in Lean, followed by kernel-checked split-prime witnesses for the finite range. The second, the determinant
  route, has no L-function, no zeta function and no zero-free theorem in its closure and needs nothing beyond Mathlib and its
  dependencies. It formalizes the argument of the companion note twinheterodox/tenth-discriminant, companions/elementary
  (commit 64694aae), which supplies the explicit constants, the choice of parameters and the overall assembly, and which builds
  on contributions of L. Silberman, K. Pratt, B. Green and R. van Dijk: Green's derivative determinant, made nonzero modulo an
  auxiliary inert prime by van Dijk's finite-field argument and divisible by Frobenius at the inert primes, rules out
  discriminants q >= 2^15 under class number one, and a kernel-checked residue sieve settles the range below. The two proofs
  share the statement, the foundational modules and the converse for the nine fields. A third entry extends the first route from
  one ideal class to every class and proves, again kernel-checked and without Heegner's method (no j-invariant or singular moduli
  occur in its closure), linear forms in logarithms or the Gross-Zagier formula, that Q(sqrt(-m)) has class number at most two
  exactly for those nine radicands and eighteen more, and class number two exactly for the eighteen: 5, 6, 10, 13, 15, 22, 35,
  37, 51, 58, 91, 115, 123, 187, 235, 267, 403, 427. The Dedekind zeta function splits into one Epstein zeta function per ideal
  class; a Chowla-Selberg formula for every positive definite form continues each to s = 7/8, and the 7/8 theorem, again proved
  inside the checked closure, forces d <= 4777 when the class number is at most two, once the positive y = 0 term of the
  non-principal class is kept. Below 4778, certificates from the principal norm form show class number at least three for the
  1,429 other fields, certificates built on a non-principal ramified prime show class number two for the eighteen, and the first
  proof gives the nine. The route is LemmaLab's extension of the Chowla-Selberg argument to all class numbers, from the comments
  of Tao's post. The theorem is classical: Baker and Stark (1971) made the problem effectively solvable, and Montgomery and
  Weinberger (1974) and Stark (1975) completed the list. Written for T. Tao's tenth-discriminant challenge (20 October 2026).
